WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.804
Aesop’s Fables. Part 11.

00:00:02.804 --> 00:00:04.724
The Ass and his Shadow.

00:00:04.724 --> 00:00:12.481
A certain man hired an Ass for a journey in summertime, and started out with the owner following behind to drive the beast.

00:00:12.481 --> 00:00:35.532
By and by, in the heat of the day, they stopped to rest, and the traveller wanted to lie down in the Ass's Shadow; but the owner, who himself wished to be out of the sun, wouldn't let him do that; for he said he had hired the Ass only, and not his Shadow: the other maintained that his bargain secured him complete control of the Ass for the time being.

00:00:35.532 --> 00:00:43.143
From words they came to blows; and while they were belabouring each other the Ass took to his heels and was soon out of sight.

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.131
The Farmer and his Sons.

00:00:45.131 --> 00:01:02.282
A Farmer, being at death's door, and desiring to impart to his Sons a secret of much moment, called them round him and said, "My sons, I am shortly about to die; I would have you know, therefore, that in my vineyard there lies a hidden treasure.

00:01:02.282 --> 00:01:04.509
Dig, and you will find it."

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:16.237
As soon as their father was dead, the Sons took spade and fork and turned up the soil of the vineyard over and over again, in their search for the treasure which they supposed to lie buried there.

00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:25.192
They found none, however: but the vines, after so thorough a digging, produced a crop such as had never before been seen.

00:01:25.192 --> 00:01:26.851
The Dog and the Cook.

00:01:26.851 --> 00:01:32.188
A rich man once invited a number of his friends and acquaintances to a banquet.

00:01:32.188 --> 00:01:46.277
His dog thought it would be a good opportunity to invite another Dog, a friend of his; so he went to him and said, "My master is giving a feast: there'll be a fine spread, so come and dine with me to-night."

00:01:46.277 --> 00:01:59.785
The Dog thus invited came, and when he saw the preparations being made in the kitchen he said to himself, "My word, I'm in luck: I'll take care to eat enough to-night to last me two or three days."

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:07.223
At the same time he wagged his tail briskly, by way of showing his friend how delighted he was to have been asked.

00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:17.666
But just then the Cook caught sight of him, and, in his annoyance at seeing a strange Dog in the kitchen, caught him up by the hind legs and threw him out of the window.

00:02:17.666 --> 00:02:23.767
He had a nasty fall, and limped away as quickly as he could, howling dismally.

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:29.911
Presently some other dogs met him, and said, "Well, what sort of a dinner did you get?"

00:02:29.911 --> 00:02:40.282
To which he replied, "I had a splendid time: the wine was so good, and I drank so much of it, that I really don't remember how I got out of the house!"

00:02:40.282 --> 00:02:43.841
Be shy of favours bestowed at the expense of others.

00:02:43.841 --> 00:02:45.405
The Monkey As King.

00:02:45.405 --> 00:02:51.946
At a gathering of all the animals the Monkey danced and delighted them so much that they made him their King.

00:02:51.946 --> 00:03:12.712
The Fox, however, was very much disgusted at the promotion of the Monkey: so having one day found a trap with a piece of meat in it, he took the Monkey there and said to him, "Here is a dainty morsel I have found, sire; I did not take it myself, because I thought it ought to be reserved for you, our King.

00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:14.805
Will you be pleased to accept it?"

00:03:14.805 --> 00:03:18.923
The Monkey made at once for the meat and got caught in the trap.

00:03:18.923 --> 00:03:31.930
Then he bitterly reproached the Fox for leading him into danger; but the Fox only laughed and said, "O Monkey, you call yourself King of the Beasts and haven't more sense than to be taken in like that!"

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:33.735
The Thieves and the Cock.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:42.371
Some Thieves broke into a house, and found nothing worth taking except a Cock, which they seized and carried off with them.

00:03:42.371 --> 00:03:59.578
When they were preparing their supper, one of them caught up the Cock, and was about to wring his neck, when he cried out for mercy and said, "Pray do not kill me: you will find me a most useful bird, for I rouse honest men to their work in the morning by my crowing."

00:03:59.578 --> 00:04:07.152
But the Thief replied with some heat, "Yes, I know you do, making it still harder for us to get a livelihood.

00:04:07.152 --> 00:04:08.923
Into the pot you go!"
